This repository explores fundamental sorting algorithms and their performance characteristics through practical implementation and analysis.  
Each algorithm is built from scratch — no built-in `sort()` or STL sorting functions — to emphasize algorithmic design, computational complexity, and real-world efficiency differences.

---

## 📘 Overview

Sorting is a cornerstone of computer science, forming the basis for search, analytics, and optimization tasks.  
Here, you’ll find clean and well-documented implementations of:

| Algorithm | Type | Best Case | Average Case | Worst Case | Space | Stable |
|------------|-------|------------|----------------|-------------|--------|---------|
|  🪣 Bucket Sort | Distribution-based | O(n + k) | O(n + k) | O(n²) | O(n + k) | Yes |
| 🔢 Radix Sort | Non-comparison | O(nk) | O(nk) | O(nk) | O(n + k) | Yes |
| ⚡ Quick Sort | Divide & Conquer | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n²) | O(log n) | No |
| ✍️ Insertion Sort | Incremental | O(n) | O(n²) | O(n²) | O(1) | Yes |
| 💨 Bubble Sort | Exchange | O(n) | O(n²) | O(n²) | O(1) | Yes |

Each algorithm was applied to a dataset of simulated phone numbers (`647-XXXXXXX`), mimicking a data-analyst’s preprocessing workflow.

